organic farming and the aftermath of the  mill quota system were the focus of the  agriculture and Fisheries come to the  gathering in Brussels on Monday March 16  the ongoing organic farming proposal  aims to review the existing legislation  on organic production I'm labeling of  products the goals are to strengthen the  development of organic production in the  EU guarantee fair competition for  farmers and improve consumer confidence  in organic goods latvia's Minister for  agriculture Janice two claps told  reporters of two chair in the meeting  that the council had a good discussion  and that the goal was to prepare ground  for the may council where ministers will  try to achieve general approach in order  to start negotiations with the European  Parliament ministers were expected to  give guidance on sensitive issues such  as the presence of pesticides as well as  other forbidden substances in organic  products they also debated the input  regime and the control systems of  organic products both of our discussions  on the import regime enabled us to  achieve considerable progress in the  development of this legislation I  believe that it is fair to consider that  all that is left to do on this issue is  just some technical adjustment as  regards controls member states have  clear stances on the extent of controls  of our own spot checks dairy was also  high on the minister's agenda as the  milk quota system that has governed the  EU market for over three decades draws  to an end in March 2015 milk quotas have  conditioned the activity of an entire  generation of dairy farmers most of whom  have never produced in an unlimited  environment as a result in some weeks  the EU mill sector will be more  market-oriented this will bring about  changes for dairy producers in a context  of price volatility and market  fluctuation the situation in the milk  market has to be followed closely  in order to be able to assess the  influence of the abolition of quotas  ministers also held a debate on the  implementation and simplification of the  Common Agricultural Policy otherwise  known as the cup finally the council was  briefed on animal welfare offered  animals following a broadcast by Dutch  TV and the publication of a report by an  animal rights organization the  Netherlands addressed the issue at  county level to prevent products  involving animal welfare malpractices  from entering the EU market
 

